**Read:** [Introdução à Mecânica dos Fluidos e aos Fenômenos de Transporte — Gobbi and colleagues](../sources/Mecanica-dos-Fluidos-e-Transportes-Gobbi-Dias.lingua#r_1F9KHVCP86X68RR7DTZASE5F1J), chapters 6–8 solute transport and balances; [Advanced fluid dynamics — Mei notes and MIT modules](../sources/Advanced-Fluid-Dynamics-Mei-MIT.lingua#r_21NXVH78F0NYDPDVTG66PG261P), chapter 4 transport and dispersion; [Microfluidics: Fluid physics at the nanoliter scale — Squires and Quake](../sources/Microfluidic-Physics-Squires-Quake.lingua#r_5TN8JGKY1B6VKGJQG859J61JE2), diffusive and reactive microtransport.

For a species mass fraction Y in a mixture, start from ∂(ρY)/∂t+∇·(ρuY+j)=ω, with diffusive flux j and production ω defined consistently. A Fickian approximation j=−ρD∇Y requires its own constitutive assumptions. In a multicomponent mixture, fluxes and reactions must respect the total mass account; independently assigning every species an incompatible diffusion flux can violate it.

Relate the conservative form to a material derivative using total continuity, then compare advection, diffusion and reaction scales. Define the Péclet and relevant Damköhler numbers from the actual length and time choices. Boundary conditions may prescribe concentration, flux or a surface reaction and are not interchangeable.

Derive a simple diffusion layer and a steady advective-reactive balance, checking nonnegative concentrations and integral closure. Connect heat and mass transfer through analogous equations only where properties and coupling justify it. Strong density, composition, phase or electric effects need an expanded model rather than a renamed heat-transfer coefficient.

**Continue:** [Mixing, stretching, diffusion and Taylor dispersion](Mixing-stretching-diffusion-and-Taylor-dispersion.lingua#r_59FDSTWHQQ1P5GFEGPR0DG3Y54).

